 2. 🤖 X becomes the AI community group chat 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Brendan Smialowski/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Elon Musk bought Twitter saying civilization needed a "common digital town square." Four years later, it's more like a live group chat for Silicon Valley's AI obsessives, Axios' Sara Fischer and Madison Mills write. 

 Why it matters: Two years ago, X was fighting off competition from a slew of new entrants. Today, it remains relevant by becoming more niche. New model releases, research papers, demos, hiring moves, high-profile executive exits and technical arguments ricochet through X in real time.

 The latest viral example came when Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon announced his resignation on X, along with a thread explaining his safety concerns. 🎨 The big picture: The AI community, particularly the researchers who define the technical progress happening within frontier AI labs, has long coalesced around ideas on X.

 Back in 2025, AI researcher Andrej Karpathy coined the term " vibe coding " in an X post, helping name one of the defining trends of the AI enterprise adoption curve. Even executives are now engaging more with the platform as a communications tool, with Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang launching his account in July alongside a post supporting open AI models. Share this story . 

 
 
